Current status (supersedes the historical stages below): hardware FROM_CPU_1
into hart1, shared FROM_CPU_3 into hart0, WFI restored. IRQ-disabled lent-idle
returns explicitly flush deferred hrtimer rearming. Routine heartbeat is
opt-in via s31_smp_diag=1; this also enables a one-shot hard-timer capture if
the CPU0 softirq heartbeat stops for six seconds. See docs/smp-ipi-plan.md
and docs/current-state.md for validation and the separate PIE experiment.

esp32s31-clic: native IPIs for CONFIG_SMP

File: drivers/irqchip/irq-esp32s31-clic.c (whole file). All new code is under
#ifdef CONFIG_SMP; the UP kernel is unchanged.

An SMP kernel IPIs itself constantly (irq_work: deferred printk, RCU, tick).
Our OpenSBI platform registers no IPI device, so with "providing IPIs using
SBI IPI extension" the first self-IPI was lost: the boot reached "console
[ttyS0] enabled", printed nothing live, and parked in arch_cpu_idle.

IPI = software-set pending bit on CLIC slot 47 (free, S-mode, EDGE, level 1),
own hart through the normal S window, other hart through +0x10000.
ipi_mux_create + riscv_ipi_set_virq_range in the probe, i.e. before
sbi_ipi_init(), which then stands down. The handler clears the latch BEFORE
ipi_mux_process() so a concurrent send re-raises instead of being wiped.

The slot must NOT have SHV: stvt is never installed on S31 (non-vectored by
design), and a vectored slot jumps to 4*id - the first attempt oopsed at
epc 0xbc = 47*4.

Not the FROM_CPU_n doorbells GrieferPig uses: all four are taken here
(0 IDF yield, 1 vCPU doorbell, 2/3 hosted). TODO stage 3: a CPU on hart0 has
emulated interrupts and is kicked through FROM_CPU_1 instead.

Verified 2026-09-20: #241 SMP boots to desktop, Wi-Fi/BT/HID up, 1 CPU.

UPDATE 2026-09-20 (the one that mattered): DEVICE SLOTS ARE HART 1's, FROM
EITHER CPU. The CLIC window is virtualised per hart, so mask/unmask/ack run on
the lent CPU wrote hart 0's CLIC and left hart 1's slot masked - the device's
interrupt was then dead for the whole boot. Which CPU ran the probe decided
it: codec I2C timing out on every transfer (3 boots in 5, ~150 x 130 ms = the
"slow boot"), console dead after one line (1 in 5). clic_readb/clic_writeb now
add the +0x10000 alias when executing on the lent CPU; one raw spinlock
replaces the per-CPU one (there is one register set); the lent CPU's own timer
and IPI are injected, so its local mask/unmask and the handler's latch clears
touch no hardware there (through the alias they would hit HART 1's timer latch
and swallow hart 1's pending IPI). set_affinity still refuses: every device
interrupt stays on hart 1. Result: 5/5 boots, 0 I2C errors, rcS complete at
32.6-36.6 s (UP ~33 s).

UPDATE: hardware doorbell migration (step 2). FROM_CPU_1/source 66 now drives
hart1 slot 47 as a LEVEL source, including self-IPIs. Clear the register and
CLIC latch before consuming mux bits. FROM_CPU_3 is shared on hart0 with
hosted transport; Linux increments the serialized SRAM request counter first.
Polling idle remains for this step; restoring WFI is a separate change.
The optional s31_smp_dump_after boot parameter emits one task snapshot; its
default is zero, so normal boots do not dump tasks.
